            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>5</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>22</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRN/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>OGCRN</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/OGCRN/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Vector Processing Unit">Vector Processing Unit</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>A core component of the <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPU architecture</fr:link> responsible for elementwise operations.</html:p>
                <html:ul><html:li>2D <fr:link href="/Q7ZD/" title="Vector ISA / SIMD" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/Q7ZD/" display-uri="Q7ZD" type="local">SIMD</fr:link> execution, where each of 1024 <html:em>sites</html:em> execute same program in lockstep.</html:li>
  <html:li>Each site contains 4 ALUs, enabling 4096 FLOPs.</html:li>
  <html:li>Fed a 8x128 <fr:link href="/OGCRO/" title="Vector Register" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRO/" display-uri="OGCRO" type="local">Vector Register</fr:link> in one cycle.</html:li></html:ul>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors>
                      <fr:author>
                        <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
                      </fr:author>
                    </fr:authors>
                    <fr:date>
                      <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                      <fr:month>8</fr:month>
                      <fr:day>10</fr:day>
                    </fr:date>
                    <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/JFWY/</fr:uri>
                    <fr:display-uri>JFWY</fr:display-uri>
                    <fr:route>/JFWY/</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="The VPU is thirty times smaller than the MXU">The VPU is thirty times smaller than the MXU</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon>
                    <fr:meta name="anki">1786395976565</fr:meta>
                    <fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p><fr:link href="/OGCRN/" title="Vector Processing Unit" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRN/" display-uri="OGCRN" type="local">Vector Processing Unit</fr:link>: what is its shape and throughput relative to the <fr:link href="/OGCRP/" title="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/" display-uri="OGCRP" type="local">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:link>, and which reductions are cheap?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  Shape <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(8\ \text {sublanes}, 128\ \text {lanes})]]></fr:tex>, with 4 independent ALUs per position and most ops at 1/cycle:

  <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[8 \times  128 \times  4 \times  1.75\mathrm {e}9 \approx  7\mathrm {e}12 \text { FLOPs/s}]]></fr:tex>

  on v5p — about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[30\times ]]></fr:tex> less than the ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex> MXU FLOPs/s per core. VPU work is fp32.

  <html:strong>Reductions</html:strong> along the <html:strong>sublane</html:strong> axis (the 8) are cheap: an intra-lane shuffle by 4/2/1 plus three pairwise adds. <html:strong>Cross-lane</html:strong> reductions need the separate XLU, which is slow and expensive.

  Control is skewed the other way: a single <fr:link href="/WY6L/" title="Scalar core (TPU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/WY6L/" display-uri="WY6L" type="local">scalar core</fr:link> fetches and dispatches everything and can issue only <html:strong>one DMA request per cycle</html:strong>. Efficient, but it blocks data-dependent vectorization.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>5</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>22</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>OGCRP</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/OGCRP/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter><html:p>A core component of the <fr:link href="/NZZ7/" title="TPU Architecture" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/NZZ7/" display-uri="NZZ7" type="local">TPU architecture</fr:link> responsible for performing matrix multiplication via a weight stationary <fr:link href="/SXP6/" title="Systolic array" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/SXP6/" display-uri="SXP6" type="local">Systolic array</fr:link>.</html:p><html:ul><html:li><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> — 16,384 <html:em>multiply accumulate</html:em> units, each multiplying and adding in one cycle. v6e/Trillium widens the array to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[256 \times  256]]></fr:tex>, i.e. 65,536.</html:li>
  <html:li>The native op is one <html:code>bf16[8,128] @ bf16[128,128] -&gt; f32[8,128]</html:code> every 8 cycles, or about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[5e13]]></fr:tex> bf16 FLOPs/s per MXU at 1.5 GHz. A v5e chip has enough MXUs to reach ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex>.</html:li></html:ul>
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(219, 216, 215);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(109, 103, 101);">Remark.</html:span> 
The side length is fixed in hardware, so every dimension has to be tiled down to it and any axis shorter than it is padded up to it, wasting the FLOPs spent on the padding. The order is
<html:ol><html:li>Tiling the matrix to load from <fr:link href="/AI2G/" title="High bandwidth memory" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/AI2G/" display-uri="AI2G" type="local">HBM</fr:link> to <fr:link href="/GFK2/" title="VMEM" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/GFK2/" display-uri="GFK2" type="local">VMEM</fr:link>.</html:li>
  <html:li>Compiler tiles from VMEM to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> tiles to load into the MXU.</html:li></html:ol>
</html:div>
<fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>8</fr:month><fr:day>10</fr:day></fr:date><fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/HPSO/</fr:uri><fr:display-uri>HPSO</fr:display-uri><fr:route>/HPSO/</fr:route><fr:title text="Why the MXU pads everything to 128">Why the MXU pads everything to 128</fr:title><fr:taxon>Card</fr:taxon><fr:meta name="anki">1786395976465</fr:meta><fr:meta name="anki-deck">MLSys</fr:meta></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  <html:p>What is the native op of one <fr:link href="/OGCRP/" title="Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/OGCRP/" display-uri="OGCRP" type="local">Matrix Multiply Unit (MXU)</fr:link>, and why does that force padding to 128?</html:p>
  
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(63, 83, 127);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(63, 83, 127);" /> 
  <html:details><html:summary><html:span class="foldout" style="font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11pt; font-weight: 500; color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.65);">Answer</html:span></html:summary>
  One <html:code>bf16[8,128] @ bf16[128,128] -&gt; f32[8,128]</html:code> every 8 cycles, on a <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times  128]]></fr:tex> systolic array — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[256 \times  256]]></fr:tex> on v6e/Trillium.

  The array's side length is fixed in hardware, so any matrix axis shorter than 128 (256 on v6e) is <html:strong>padded</html:strong> up to it, and the FLOPs spent on the padding are simply wasted. With 2–4 MXUs per core, tiling dimensions want to exceed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[128 \times ]]></fr:tex> the MXU count.

  Scale: roughly <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[5e13]]></fr:tex> bf16 FLOPs/s per MXU at 1.5 GHz, so about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2e14]]></fr:tex> per v5e chip. int8 runs ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[2\times ]]></fr:tex> faster, int4 ~<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[4\times ]]></fr:tex>.
</html:details>
</html:div>



</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree></fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
            </fr:authors>
            <fr:date>
              <fr:year>2026</fr:year>
              <fr:month>3</fr:month>
              <fr:day>23</fr:day>
            </fr:date>
            <fr:uri>https://kellenkanarios.com/060Z/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>060Z</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/060Z/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Pallas">Pallas</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>3</fr:month><fr:day>23</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Why Custom Kernels?">Why Custom Kernels?</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
  
  
 <html:div class="jms-callout" style="
  margin: 1.1rem 0;
  padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 0.9rem;
  border-left: 2px solid rgb(38, 97, 69);
  "><html:span style="font-weight: 500; color: rgb(38, 97, 69);">Example.</html:span> 
  In attention kernel, we perform <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[QK^T]]></fr:tex> in VMEM then copy back to HBM, then copy back to VMEM and do the softmax. Instead we want to <html:em>fuse</html:em> this into one kernel.
  </html:div>


  <html:figure><html:img width="100%" src="/bafkrmifn52vzltvmavkzqh7sj2oluirqgq2hpkxzldgkwhp2j2mbahh5ei.png" />
  <html:figcaption>Summary of XLA fusions for attention kernel.</html:figcaption></html:figure>
  <html:ul><html:li>Logit &amp; Max fusion:</html:li> 
    <html:ul><html:li>34.45% flop utilization</html:li> 
      <html:ul><html:li>Use MXU to get over 350TFLOP/s.</html:li></html:ul>
      <html:li>18.74% HBM bandwidth utilization.</html:li>  
      <html:li>3.55% On-chip read utilization</html:li></html:ul>
    <html:li>Softmax denom:</html:li>
    <html:ul><html:li>0.32% flop utilization.</html:li>
      <html:li>0.0% HBM bandwidth utilization.</html:li>
      <html:ul><html:li>Keeps 1024x1024 logit matrix resident in VMEM.</html:li></html:ul>
      <html:li>22.61% On-chip read utilization</html:li>
      <html:ul><html:li>Uses VPU to compute softmax which consumes data from VMEM at 6.6 TB/s.</html:li></html:ul></html:ul>
    <html:li>Attention output:</html:li>
    <html:ul><html:li>35.82% flop utilization.</html:li>
      <html:li>19.49% HBM bandwidth utilization.</html:li>
      <html:li>7.39% On-chip read utilization.</html:li></html:ul></html:ul>
  <html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">HBM Bottleneck.</html:strong> The default XLA attention does not fuse the softmax with the logit computation, requiring the entire logit matrix to be written back to HBM.</html:p>
  <html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Vector Unit Limitations.</html:strong> MXU can be left idle while VPU is performing copmlex exponential and sum operations.</html:p>
  <html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">VMEM Residency and Loop Control.</html:strong> Pallas allows you to control data movement, so that we can explicitly enforce <html:em>online softmax</html:em> rather than writing back to HBM.</html:p>
  <html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Temporal Control via LLO.</html:strong> Pallas also allows control over the <html:em>temporal flow</html:em>, preventing the compiler from reordering your manual optimizations.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>3</fr:month><fr:day>23</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Pallas Fundamentals">Pallas Fundamentals</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:em>Ignore the HLO pass.</html:em>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Writing a kernel.</html:strong></html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Inside the kernel, data has already been moved from HBM to VMEM.
<html:figure><html:img width="100%" src="/bafkrmiha7cfu4qisvm3es3cn2gvqjz7u3l5o6piqj3lehpbl34j5bf42ma.png" /></html:figure></html:li>
  <html:li>If data exceeds VMEM, we need to <html:em>tile</html:em> with <html:code>BlockSpec</html:code>.</html:li>
  <html:li>Also need to <html:em>pipeline</html:em> i.e. while doing computation on a given block, we need to be moving the next block from HBM to VMEM.
  <html:figure><html:img width="100%" src="/bafkrmickz6lveguktbndsvjizvsm6h6cbezis3bext7ownzoczk5sqosei.png" /></html:figure></html:li>
  <html:li>By default, pipelining is done for you, but there are other APIs to control this(?)</html:li>
  <html:ul><html:li>For-Loop scheduler.</html:li>
    <html:li>Asynchronous overlap.</html:li>
    <html:li>Buffer reuse. Uses same VMEM buffer across iterations.</html:li></html:ul></html:ul>
  <html:figure><html:img width="100%" src="/bafkrmieaepdhrv4tueesd6qhpu2sfijzhjoeyqs2i7xtryiimjhaqvgdeu.png" /></html:figure>
  <html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Two-level Tiling.</html:strong></html:p>
  <html:ul><html:li>Macro-tiling: this is done by Pallas / user. You define the larger software blocks i.e. 1024x1024 to hide latency DMA transfers. Pallas manages pipeling.</html:li>
    <html:li>Micro-tiling: this is done by compiler / hardware. Translates larger block into hardware native VREG 8x128.
    </html:li></html:ul>
  <html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">VPU Pipelining.</html:strong></html:p>
  <html:ul><html:li>VLOAD: fetching inputs for <html:strong>chunk (8x128)(N + 1)th</html:strong> from VMEM and move to VRegs</html:li>
    <html:li>VALU: Perform elementwise operations for <html:strong>current (Nth)</html:strong> chunk </html:li>
    <html:li>VSTORE: write completed result for <html:strong>previous (N - 1)th</html:strong> chunk from VRegs back to VMEM.</html:li></html:ul>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>3</fr:month><fr:day>23</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Flash Attention">Flash Attention</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Online softmax.</html:strong></html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Each block does a local softmax calculation.</html:li>
  <html:li>Tracks a shared maximum and adjusts results.</html:li></html:ul>
<html:p>Can Now fuse score calculation, softmax, and value aggregation into single pass.</html:p>
<html:p>No longer need to store NxN attention matrix in HBM.</html:p>
<html:p>Can break <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Q, K, V]]></fr:tex> into small blocks that fit in VMEM.</html:p>
<html:p>Kernel available <fr:link href="https://github.com/jax-ml/jax/blob/main/jax/experimental/pallas/ops/tpu/flash_attention.py" type="external">here</fr:link>.</html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Dynamic scaling <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\alpha )]]></fr:tex>: Computes <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e^{m_{prev} - m_{next}}]]></fr:tex> to track change in maximum logit across blocks for numerical stability.</html:li>
  <html:li>Re-scale accumulation: Multiplies by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\alpha ]]></fr:tex> to the previously accumulated sums before adding current block output to accurately rescale all past results.</html:li>
  <html:li>Additive updates: partial results (current softmax and outputs) are added to running totals via fast multiply and addition ops.</html:li>
  <html:li>Delayed normalization: only divide by normalization once at the very end of the loop because division is slow.</html:li></html:ul>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Block-size tuning.</html:strong> to maximize compute-to-load ratio need biggest block sizes that fit into VMEM.</html:p>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>

  <fr:tree show-metadata="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link href="/kellenkanarios/" title="Kellen Kanarios" uri="https://kellenkanarios.com/kellenkanarios/" display-uri="kellenkanarios" type="local">Kellen Kanarios</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:date><fr:year>2026</fr:year><fr:month>3</fr:month><fr:day>23</fr:day></fr:date><fr:title text="Splash Attention">Splash Attention</fr:title></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter>
<html:p>With certain masks (i.e. causal) can skip alot of computation for parts of the attention matrix that are being masked out.</html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Finer blocks -&gt; can skip more but then have poorer utilization.</html:li></html:ul>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Pre-processing Mask Info.</html:strong></html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Sparsity pruning: Divide mask into blocks and discard all inactive blocks (all zeros).</html:li>
  <html:li>Data compression: see how many unique mask patterns are needed to describe the remaining active blocks.</html:li>
  <html:li>Execution map and pre-fetching: (active_rows, mask_next) generated to map the computation order and tell the MXU which mask pattern to prefetch for zero-latency application.</html:li></html:ul>
<html:p><html:strong style="font-family: var(--serif); font-weight: 400; font-style: italic;">Metadata structures.</html:strong></html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Block filtering (block_mask): categorizes QK blocks as zero, non-zero, partial.</html:li>
  <html:li>Coordinate mapping (active_ros/cols): provides the hardware with (Q, KV) grid indices, ensuring efficient loading of active blocks from HBM.</html:li>
  <html:li>Mask pipelining (mask_next): tells the hardware which mask pattern to prefetch.</html:li>
  <html:li>SMEM-resident metadata: computed once per mask and stored in SMEM. Enable high-speed access to sparse execution map without need to re-transfer from HBM.</html:li></html:ul>
<html:p><html:strong>Pallas Call</html:strong></html:p>
<html:ul><html:li>Grid definition: defined by (num_q_heads, grid_size). Grid size is set dynamically using the sparese meta data <html:code>fwd_mask_info.num_active_blocks[0]</html:code>.</html:li>
  <html:li>SMEM Inputs: <html:code>num_scalar_prefetch</html:code> instructs hardware to prefetch critical scalar/metadata inputs into SMEM before thread begins computing.</html:li>
  <html:li>VMEM Inputs: (Q, K, V) that must be moved from HBM to VMEM for kernel to execute.</html:li></html:ul>
</fr:mainmatter></fr:tree>
</fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
